    ],
    "justification": "The rate comparison turns the minimax theorem into an exact answer about when nuisance learning destroys oracle pointwise precision.",
    "gap": "The oracle elbow appears in KennedyBalakrishnanRobinsWasserman2024MinimaxCATE, but its necessity on the exact-uniform subclass requires the new same-class converse.",
    "consumer": "The classification supplies the decision-facing phase boundary for interpreting pointwise precision in R-learner and causal-forest workflows.",
    "proof_tex": "By the definitions of \\(\\eta\\), \\(S_\\star\\), and \\(\\rho_n^{\\mathrm{or}}\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{split}\n\\eta<\\frac{\\gamma}{2\\gamma+d}\n&\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n1+\\frac d{2\\gamma}+\\frac d{2S}>2+\\frac d\\gamma\\\\\n&\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\frac d{2S}>1+\\frac d{2\\gamma}\\\\\n&\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nS<\\frac{d\\gamma}{2\\gamma+d}=S_\\star.\n\\end{split} \\tag{44}\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathrm{ass:below\\mbox{-}elbow}\\) makes\n\\(\\delta=\\gamma/(2\\gamma+d)-\\eta\\) strictly positive.  The two-sided conclusion of \\(\\mathrm{thm:sharp\\mbox{-}exact\\mbox{-}uniform\\mbox{-}rate}\\) then gives constants independent of \\(n\\) such that\n\\[\n\\frac{R_n}{\\rho_n^{\\mathrm{or}}}\n\\asymp\\frac{n^{-\\eta}}{n^{-\\gamma/(2\\gamma+d)}}\n=n^\\delta\\longrightarrow\\infty.\n\\]\nHence no tuple in the stipulated strict below-elbow regime has the oracle order.  If one substitutes the boundary value \\(S=S_\\star\\) into (44), equality holds, and both displayed powers are exactly \\(n^{-\\gamma/(2\\gamma+d)}\\).  Since both rate definitions are pure powers, this boundary equality introduces no logarithmic factor.",
